        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Thermopylae</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">480 BCE, between July and September; the Persian army was sighted in mid-August, and the fighting lasted three days</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The pass of Thermopylae, on the Malian Gulf, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; the pass is now several kilometres inland because of sedimentation</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">An alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a>, about 7,000 men by the usual modern reckoning, of whom an estimated 300 were Spartans; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>, with modern estimates of the whole army generally between 120,000 and 300,000, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives 2.6 million</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> forced the pass after a mountain path was revealed to them, and the Greek rearguard was killed, apart from the Thebans, most of whom reportedly surrendered. The Persians went on to overrun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> and take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
