    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ancient and modern figures for the Persian army are far apart: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives 2.6 million military personnel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> 800,000, and modern scholars generally 120,000 to 300,000.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> attacked the Greek position frontally on two days without success, and passed it only when a local man, Ephialtes, told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> of the mountain path.</li><li>Thermopylae was a defeat for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in the war, but the stand of the rearguard became one of the most remembered episodes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A hill on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> were thought to have died was identified differently after 1939, when the archaeologist Spyridon Marinatos found large numbers of Persian bronze arrowheads on Kolonos Hill.</p></aside>
    </section>

