    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Persian expedition destroys Eretria but is met at Marathon by a heavily outnumbered Athenian army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> withdraw to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">481 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A congress at Corinth forms a confederate alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Spring 480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> leave the Vale of Tempe and adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>'s plan to hold Thermopylae on land and the straits of Artemisium at sea</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Mid-August 480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Persian army is sighted across the Malian Gulf, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas</a> refuses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>'s demand that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> hand over their arms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Persian assaults on the first two days fail, and Ephialtes tells <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> of the mountain path</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">On the third day the Greek rearguard is surrounded and killed and the pass is opened</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late 480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Greek fleet defeats the Persian fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">479 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Greek army defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a>, ending the invasion</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-thermopylae" data-v="63a1a30330" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Greeks hold the middle gate</b> Leonidas camped at the narrowest part of the pass, where the Phocians had built a wall, and posted 1,000 Phocians on the heights above. The Persian army came up across the Malian Gulf.</li><li><b>Xerxes's archers shoot first</b> On the first day Xerxes ordered 5,000 archers to shoot a barrage of arrows. They shot from at least 100 yards away, and the Greeks' wooden shields and bronze helmets deflected the arrows.</li><li><b>The Medes attack the Greek line</b> Xerxes sent 10,000 Medes and Cissians forward, and the Persians launched a frontal assault in waves of about 10,000 men. The Greeks fought in front of the Phocian wall, at the narrowest part of the pass.</li><li><b>The Immortals fare no better</b> Xerxes threw the Immortals into a second assault the same day, and they made no headway. On the second day the Persian infantry again attacked the pass without success.</li><li><b>Xerxes withdraws to his camp</b> Xerxes stopped the assault and withdrew to his camp, as the Persians had had no more success than on the first day.</li><li><b>Ephialtes shows the mountain path</b> A Trachinian named Ephialtes told Xerxes of the mountain path around Thermopylae. Hydarnes set out that evening with the Immortals to encircle the Greeks.</li><li><b>The Phocians see the Persian column</b> The Phocians guarding the path became aware of the outflanking Persian column. They retreated to a nearby hill to make their stand, assuming the Persians had come to attack them.</li><li><b>The Persians bypass the Phocians</b> Not wishing to be delayed, the Persians merely shot a volley of arrows at the Phocians. They then went on past them with the encirclement of the main Greek force.</li><li><b>Leonidas lets his allies leave</b> Learning that his army had been encircled, Leonidas told his allies that they could leave if they wanted to. Many did, and around 2,000 soldiers stayed behind.</li><li><b>The Greeks sally from the wall</b> A Persian force of 10,000 light infantry and cavalry charged the front of the Greek formation. The Spartans and Thespians marched out from the wall to meet it in the wider part of the pass. Leonidas was killed.</li><li><b>The Greeks withdraw to the hill</b> As the Immortals approached, the Greeks withdrew from the wall and took a stand on a hill behind it. The Spartans and Thespians moved there together.</li><li><b>The Thebans surrender</b> The Thebans moved away from their companions and advanced towards the Persians with their hands upraised. A few were killed before their surrender was accepted.</li><li><b>The last stand on the hill</b> Xerxes had part of the wall torn down and the hill surrounded, and the Persians shot arrows until every last Greek was dead.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria had aided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against the Persian Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in 499–494 BCE, and Darius vowed to punish those involved, especially the Athenians. In 491 BCE he asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> for earth and water as tokens of submission. Most gave them, but in Athens the ambassadors were put on trial and thrown into a pit, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> they were thrown down a well. The expedition of 490 BCE under Datis and Artaphernes destroyed Eretria, but at Marathon it met a heavily outnumbered Athenian army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> withdrew to Asia.</p><p>Darius began raising a new army, but his Egyptian province revolted in 486 BCE, and he died while preparing to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> crushed the revolt and prepared a full-scale invasion with stockpiling and conscription, bridging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and having a canal dug across the isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. In early 480 BCE the army mustered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> crossed the Hellespont on two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridges</a>, and many Greek cities gave earth and water.</p><p>Athens had been building a large fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> since 482 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>. Xerxes deliberately left Athens and Sparta out of his demand, and support gathered around them. A congress at Corinth in late autumn 481 BCE formed an alliance of Greek city-states. A force of 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> sent to the Vale of Tempe withdrew after Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> warned that the vale could be bypassed. Themistocles then proposed holding the pass of Thermopylae while the allied navies blocked the straits of Artemisium.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Persian numbers have been disputed at length. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> claimed 2.6 million military personnel, with as many support personnel; the contemporary poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> speaks of four million, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> gave 800,000 as the total assembled. Modern scholars tend to reject these figures as miscalculations or exaggerations by the victors and generally estimate 120,000 to 300,000. The numbers at Thermopylae are as uncertain, since it is unclear whether the whole army marched there or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> left garrisons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a>, one of the Spartan kings, marched ahead of the main Spartan army, which was held back by the festival of Carneia and the Olympic truce. He took the 300 men of the royal bodyguard, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippeis">Hippeis</a>; Herodotus says he chose only men with living sons. Contingents joined on the way, and the force numbered more than 7,000 at the pass. The sources differ on its makeup: Diodorus gives 1,000 Lacedemonians and 3,000 other Peloponnesians, while Herodotus tallies 3,100 Peloponnesians in one passage and gives 4,000 in another. Many modern historians add 1,000 Lacedemonians and 900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> to Herodotus's 5,200 for about 7,000, though other combinations are plausible.</p><p>Leonidas camped at the middle gate, the narrowest part of the pass, where the Phocians had built a wall. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> could block it with no risk of being outflanked by cavalry. The weak point was a mountain track across the highland, which people from Trachis told Leonidas of, and he stationed 1,000 Phocians on the heights.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Persian army was sighted across the Malian Gulf in mid-August. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> sent an emissary offering freedom, the title "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Persian People" and better land. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas</a> refused, and to the written demand to hand over arms he is reported to have answered "Molṑn labé", usually translated "come and take them". Xerxes waited four days for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to disperse. On the first day of battle he ordered 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> to shoot, to little effect, then sent 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and Cissians in frontal assaults. The Greeks fought before the Phocian wall, each city's unit kept together and rotated. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> the first wave was "cut to ribbons", with only two or three Spartans killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and Diodorus say Xerxes then sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immortals">Immortals</a>, 10,000 men, who fared no better.</p><p>The second day went no better. Later that day Ephialtes, a Trachinian seeking a reward, told Xerxes of the path. Herodotus reports that he sent Hydarnes with the Immortals that evening; Diodorus gives Hydarnes 20,000 men. At daybreak on the third day the Phocians on the path noticed the column by the rustling of oak leaves, withdrew to a hill, and were bypassed after a volley of arrows.</p><p>Leonidas called a council of war at dawn. Many Greeks left, and around 2,000 stayed. The 700 Thespians under Demophilus refused to go, and the 400 Thebans and probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> also remained. Herodotus admits doubt whether the others withdrew without orders or at Leonidas's command. The Greeks advanced into the wider part of the pass and fought with spears until they broke, then with short swords. Leonidas was shot down by archers and the two sides fought over his body. The rest withdrew to a hill behind the wall, the Thebans surrendered, and the others were shot down with arrows.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea.jpg" alt="Battle of Plataea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Plataea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Steeple Davis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the pass was opened to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at a cost of up to 20,000 dead. The Greek rearguard was annihilated, with a probable loss of 2,000 including the dead of the first two days. Herodotus at one point says 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> died, a figure that is probably too high. The king later had the Theban prisoners branded with the royal mark. The Greeks buried their dead on the hill, and forty years after the battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas</a>'s bones were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and buried with full honours, with funeral games held in his memory every year.</p><p>The simultaneous naval battle at Artemisium had been a tactical stalemate, and with the pass lost the Greek fleet retreated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a>, where it helped ferry the remaining Athenians to Salamis. The Persian army sacked and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Thespiae and took the evacuated city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The Peloponnesians demolished the road across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> and built a wall. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> persuaded the Greeks to seek a decisive victory against the Persian fleet, and at Salamis in late 480 BCE they destroyed much of it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> withdrew with much of his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, reportedly losing many of his troops to starvation and disease, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> to continue the campaign. The next year a Greek army defeated Mardonius at the Battle of Plataea, ending the invasion, and the Greeks destroyed much of the remaining Persian fleet at Mycale.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The principal ancient account is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-general">Pausanias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>'s figures for the first wave and the whole army. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a>, a contemporary, wrote epigrams commemorating the dead, the earliest known appearance of the battle in culture. The accounts disagree on numbers, as set out above, and modern historians, who usually consider Herodotus more reliable, build their figures on his. Herodotus himself admits doubt about whether most of the Greek contingents left on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas</a>'s order. Herodotus also reports a consultation of the Oracle at Delphi, and the anecdotes about Leonidas and the Spartans, such as his exchange with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgo-queen-of-sparta">Gorgo</a>, are described as apocryphal and not verifiable.</p><p>Historians also dispute the Thebans. Herodotus suggests they were hostages to ensure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>'s good behaviour; Plutarch argued that hostages would have been sent away with the rest, and the likelier explanation is that they were Theban loyalists who objected to Persian domination. Archaeology bears on the site. The pass is often said to have been so narrow that only one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> could pass, but recent core samples indicate it was about 100 metres wide, with the sea reaching the gates. In 1939 Marinatos found Persian arrowheads on Kolonos Hill, which changed the identification of the hill of the last stand.</p><p>Why Leonidas stayed is also debated. It is said that he obeyed Spartan law, that he acted on the Oracle's words, or, in a commonly accepted theory, that he formed a rearguard so that the others could get away, which could save more than 3,000 men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within the Persian invasion Thermopylae was undoubtedly a defeat for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and forcing the pass a strategic Persian victory, though the retreat of most of the Greek troops was in its way a victory too. The battle showed that the Greeks could fight effectively against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and the defeat made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas</a> and his men martyrs, which raised Greek morale. It is disputed that the stand bought much time before Salamis: about one month passed, and George Cawkwell suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>'s own delay in reducing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> explains the gap. Cawkwell speaks of a "brilliant success" for Xerxes, and Lazenby calls the Greek defeat "disastrous".</p><p>The fame of the battle rests chiefly on the example it set. Contemporary Greeks saw in it a contrast between a small free people and imperial subjects, a view that can be seen as a sweeping overgeneralisation. A well-known epigram, usually attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a>, was engraved on a stone above the burial mound of the Spartans: "O stranger, tell the Lacedaemonians that we lie here, obedient to their words". The original stone has not survived; a new one was engraved in 1955. The site has a Leonidas Monument by Vassos Falireas, with the words "Molṑn labé", and a monument to the 700 Thespians, unveiled in 1997.</p><p>Interest in the battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> revived in the 1700s with the poems Leonidas by Richard Glover (1737) and by Willem van Haren (1742). Steven Pressfield's novel Gates of Fire (1998) is unusual in depicting the battle as gruesome. Zack Snyder's film 300 (2006), based on Frank Miller's comic series, grossed US$456 million globally. The Battle of the Persian Gate in 330 BCE has been called "the Persian Thermopylae".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Thermopylae in 480 BCE a Greek force under the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> blocked the pass against the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> for several days, until Persian troops reached their rear by a mountain path and the Greek rearguard was killed. It was a Persian victory in the campaign, and the stand became widely remembered.</em></p>

